## What to deliver

Build and rig the Breaker up operation by operation until each `parts/<part>.png` matches
the brief, the assembled `scene/` previews show the parts fitting together correctly, and
the rig is complete: a wide, top-heavy obsidian biped with a brick-slab torso on two short
thick legs, two enormous fused ram-forearms it walks knuckled over, a low ram-head sunk
between the shoulders, acid-green seams cracking across the chest, and a contiguous accent
region on the chest slab and the ram-forearms' outer faces sculpted **only** in `#4a4358`
and appearing nowhere else on the model. **Author the two required animations** as
F-curves with the `voxel-anim` animation subcommands: a **`move`** (a heavy, slow trudge
whose mass rocks side to side over each planted foot, authored in place) and an
**`attack`** (a full-body ram that rears and drives both forearms down and forward, then
recovers, while the feet stay planted). The animations and the accent region are the fixed
contract — produce all of them; the parts and joints that realize them are yours. You may
add your own extra parts, joints, or animations on top, but do not drop or contradict the
required `move` and `attack`. Before you finish, run `voxel-anim render` so it emits the
per-part `.glb` geometry your result is built from — an unrendered part is scored as empty
(`voxel-anim render --component <part>` redraws one part; `voxel-anim render --time <ms>
--animation <name>` renders the model posed at that instant of an animation to check the
motion). When you are satisfied, stop: the recorded per-part logs and `rig.json` are your
finished submission.
